---
import Layout from '../../layouts/Layout.astro';
import GuideNav from './_GuideNav.astro';
import { guides } from './_guides.ts';

const guide = guides.find(item => item.path === 'build-checks/')!;
---

<Layout title={guide.title} description={guide.description}>
  <main>
    <h1>{guide.title}</h1>
    <p>{guide.description}</p>
    <p>
      <code>astro build</code> turns your pages into HTML, but it does not run
      the type checker, does not run tests and does not look at the result. A
      page with a missing description, or with social tags that disagree with
      its canonical URL, builds without complaint. The starter adds checks around the build so
      those mistakes stop a deployment instead of reaching search engines.
    </p>

    <section aria-labelledby="verify">
      <h2 id="verify">One command: <code>pnpm verify</code></h2>
      <p>
        Run <code>pnpm verify</code> before you commit, with your production
        <code>site</code> configured. It runs six steps in order and stops at the
        first failure:
      </p>
      <ol>
        <li><code>pnpm check</code> runs <code>astro check</code>: Astro diagnostics and TypeScript in its strictest preset, across pages, components and scripts.</li>
        <li><code>pnpm test</code> runs the unit tests with Node's built-in test runner: metadata resolution, URL rules, the build receipt, the output checks and title-card drawing.</li>
        <li><code>pnpm test:build</code> builds small fixture projects with the real Astro CLI and checks the rendered metadata, the sitemap selection and title cards across output formats and subpaths.</li>
        <li><code>pnpm build</code> builds your site into <code>dist/</code>.</li>
        <li><code>pnpm verify:output</code> checks that output.</li>
        <li><code>pnpm lint:html</code> audits the HTML with deadhead.</li>
      </ol>
      <p>
        One build test removes the title-card feature and its package the way
        the README describes, so it may need registry access even after a
        frozen install.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="build">
      <h2 id="build">Checks inside the build</h2>
      <p>
        Some rules run during <code>pnpm build</code> itself, so they protect
        every build, including one started by a host. The build fails when
        <code>site</code> is missing or includes a path, credentials, a query or a
        fragment; when two routes would write the same file; when a title card
        cannot be drawn, for example because the font lacks a glyph; or when the
        sitemap the official integration wrote does not match the pages selected
        for it. A failed build also deletes the previous build record, so the
        later checks cannot pass against stale output.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="output">
      <h2 id="output">What <code>verify:output</code> checks</h2>
      <p>
        A successful build records its origin, base, output directory and the
        pages it emitted in <code>.astro/starter-build.json</code>. The output
        check reads that record, so a build with <code>--site</code>,
        <code>--base</code> or <code>--outDir</code> is checked where it actually
        went. For every page that uses the layout it confirms:
      </p>
      <ul>
        <li>exactly one nonblank title and description, and a nonblank language;</li>
        <li>an absolute HTTP(S) canonical URL without a fragment;</li>
        <li>a complete social block whose title, description and URL agree with the page, with <code>summary_large_image</code> as the card type;</li>
        <li>a generated title card that decodes as a 1200×630 PNG and belongs to the page that names it;</li>
        <li>no internal build markers left in the HTML.</li>
      </ul>
      <p>
        It then compares <code>robots.txt</code> and every sitemap file with the
        recorded selection. Your own CSS, scripts and fonts are allowed; pages
        that bypass the layout keep their own metadata rules.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="deadhead">
      <h2 id="deadhead">The deadhead HTML audit</h2>
      <p>
        <a href="https://deadhead.cevdet.ch/install">deadhead</a> lints HTML for
        deprecated, unnecessary and harmful markup. The pinned version, 0.3.1,
        has 203 rules, covering problems such as a
        missing viewport tag, <code>rel="shortcut icon"</code> or a keywords meta
        tag that no major search engine reads. Every finding links to an
        explanation with sources. <code>pnpm lint:html</code> runs the published
        CLI over the whole recorded output directory, including HTML copied
        from <code>public/</code>, with its default rules and threshold: any
        finding fails the step. It changes no files, applies no fixes and keeps
        no baseline, so what you see is what ships.
      </p>
    </section>

    <section aria-labelledby="ci">
      <h2 id="ci">In CI and on the host</h2>
      <p>
        The GitHub Actions workflow runs the same steps on Node 24.8.0 and the
        latest LTS release, with a frozen pnpm install and Actions pinned to
        commits. It builds with <code>https://example.test</code> as the origin,
        so your own <code>site</code> stays yours to set. On the host, chain the
        checks after the build:
      </p>
      <pre><code>{'pnpm build --site https://example.com && pnpm verify:output && pnpm lint:html'}</code></pre>
      <p>
        If either check fails, the host gets a failing command and keeps the
        previous deployment. The <a href="../cloudflare-pages/">Cloudflare Pages
        guide</a> shows where that command goes.
      </p>
    </section>

    <GuideNav current={guide.path} />
  </main>
</Layout>
